from tn import *
def C(ch,*a,**k): TN(10,"science",ch,*a,**k)
C("structural-organisation-of-animals","In the lab Raju saw a preserved leech and a rabbit model. \"Their bodies show how organ systems work,\" said his teacher.",
 ["The Indian cattle leech lives in freshwater and sucks blood. The chapter studies its external form, the divisions of its body and the body wall, how it moves with suckers, and its digestive, respiratory, circulatory, excretory, nervous and reproductive systems.",
  "The rabbit is a mammal with a body cavity, the coelom. It has a digestive system suited to eating plants, a respiratory system with lungs, a circulatory system with a four-chambered heart, an excretory system with kidneys, a nervous system and a reproductive system.",
  "Comparing the two animals shows how structure fits the way of life, and how organ systems work together in an animal's body."],
 ["leech","rabbit","coelom","digestive system","circulation","nervous system"],
 ("Which animal in this chapter is a mammal?",["Mammals have hair and feed milk to young.","The rabbit has these features.","So the rabbit."]),
 ("The leech is a mammal.",["The leech is an annelid worm.","The rabbit is the mammal."]),
 ["The leech is a blood-sucking annelid.","The rabbit is a mammal with a coelom.","Organ systems work together.","Structure suits habitat."],
 ["m|The Indian cattle leech is a|blood-sucking worm|mammal|bird|It lives in freshwater.","t|The rabbit has a coelom.|T|Yes.","m|The rabbit's body cavity is the|coelom|nucleus|stomach|It holds organs.","t|The rabbit is a reptile.|F|It is a mammal.","m|The leech moves using|suckers|wings|fins|It attaches and loops.","s|Which organ pumps blood in a rabbit?|heart|The heart.","m|Mammal kidneys are part of the|excretory system|nervous system|skeleton|They filter blood.","t|Structure of an animal suits its way of life.|T|Yes."])
C("transportation-in-plants-and-circulation-in-animals","Meena watched a coloured flower take up inked water. \"Water travels up the stem,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Materials move in cells by diffusion, from higher to lower concentration, by osmosis, the movement of water through a semi-permeable membrane, and by active transport, which uses energy to move substances against a concentration gradient. Root hairs take in water and minerals.",
  "Water moves across the root by the apoplast and symplast pathways, and it rises in the xylem by root pressure, transpiration pull and the cohesion of water, which is the ascent of sap. Transpiration is loss of water vapour from leaves. Phloem translocates sugars from the leaves to other parts.",
  "In animals blood circulates in blood vessels, which are arteries carrying blood away from the heart, veins bringing it back and capillaries joining them. The heart pumps the blood, and the chapter describes blood, the heart's working and lymph."],
 ["diffusion","osmosis","active transport","transpiration pull","phloem transport","arteries and veins"],
 ("Name the tissue that transports sugars in plants.",["Sugars made in leaves must reach other parts.","The tissue for food transport is phloem.","This is translocation."]),
 ("Arteries bring blood back to the heart.",["Arteries carry blood away from the heart.","Veins bring it back."]),
 ["Diffusion goes from high to low concentration.","Xylem carries water up.","Phloem carries food.","Arteries away, veins back."],
 ["m|Movement of water through a semi-permeable membrane is|osmosis|diffusion|digestion|Water moves.","t|Active transport needs energy.|T|Yes.","m|Ascent of sap is through the|xylem|phloem|stomata|It is upward water flow.","t|Veins carry blood away from the heart.|F|Arteries do.","m|Loss of water from leaves is|transpiration|translocation|diffusion|Through stomata.","s|Which vessels join arteries to veins?|capillaries|Capillaries.","m|Translocation of sugars is through|phloem|xylem|roots|Food from leaves.","t|Root hairs absorb water.|T|Yes."])
C("nervous-system","Asha touched a hot pan and pulled her hand away before she even thought. \"That is a reflex,\" said her teacher.",
 ["The nervous system controls and coordinates body activities. Its unit is the neuron, made of the cell body, dendrites and an axon. Neurons are sensory, motor or association neurons, and nerve fibres can be myelinated or non-myelinated. An impulse passes along a neuron as an electrical signal and across a synapse with chemicals called neurotransmitters.",
  "The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord. The brain is protected by the skull and bathed in cerebrospinal fluid. A reflex action is a quick automatic response to a stimulus, controlled by the spinal cord through a reflex arc.",
  "The peripheral nervous system has cranial and spinal nerves, and the autonomic nervous system controls involuntary actions like heartbeat through its sympathetic and parasympathetic parts."],
 ["neuron","synapse","neurotransmitter","central nervous system","reflex action","autonomic system"],
 ("Name the two parts of the central nervous system.",["The central part commands the body.","It is made of the brain.","And the spinal cord."]),
 ("Reflex actions are controlled by the cerebrum.",["Reflexes are quick, spinal cord responses.","They do not wait for thinking."]),
 ["The neuron is the unit.","CNS = brain + spinal cord.","Reflexes are automatic.","Autonomic controls involuntary actions."],
 ["m|The unit of the nervous system is the|neuron|nephron|alveolus|A nerve cell.","t|A reflex action is quick and automatic.|T|Yes.","m|The CNS includes the|brain and spinal cord|heart and lungs|liver|Central.","t|Neurotransmitters work at a synapse.|T|Yes.","m|Involuntary actions are controlled by the|autonomic system|skeleton|skin|Like heartbeat.","s|What protects and cushions the brain? Two words.|cerebrospinal fluid|CSF.","m|The axon|carries impulses away|stores food|digests|From the cell body.","t|Sensory neurons carry impulses to the CNS.|T|Yes."])
C("plant-and-animal-hormones","Raju saw a farmer ripening bananas with a gas. \"A plant hormone, ethylene!\" said his teacher.",
 ["Hormones are chemical messengers made in small amounts. Plant hormones include auxins, which cause cell elongation; cytokinins, which promote cell division; gibberellins, which help stem growth; abscisic acid, a growth inhibitor; and ethylene, which ripens fruits.",
  "In humans, hormones are made by the endocrine glands, which pour them directly into the blood. The pituitary is the master gland. The thyroid makes thyroxine, the parathyroid controls calcium, the pancreas makes insulin, the adrenal makes adrenaline and the gonads make sex hormones, while the thymus helps immunity.",
  "Hormones must be balanced. Too little insulin causes diabetes and a lack of iodine can cause goitre by affecting the thyroid."],
 ["auxin","gibberellin","ethylene","endocrine gland","pituitary","insulin"],
 ("Which hormone ripens fruits?",["It is a plant hormone.","It is a gas.","Ethylene."]),
 ("The pancreas makes thyroxine.",["Thyroxine comes from the thyroid.","The pancreas makes insulin."]),
 ["Hormones are chemical messengers.","Ethylene ripens fruits.","The pituitary is the master gland.","Insulin lowers blood sugar."],
 ["m|The master gland is the|pituitary|thyroid|adrenal|It controls other glands.","t|Auxins help cell elongation.|T|Yes.","m|Insulin is made by the|pancreas|liver|thyroid|It controls blood sugar.","t|Endocrine glands have ducts.|F|They pour hormones into the blood directly.","m|A growth-inhibiting plant hormone is|abscisic acid|auxin|cytokinin|ABA.","s|Which gland makes thyroxine?|thyroid|The thyroid.","m|Lack of iodine can cause|goitre|fever|cold|It affects the thyroid.","t|Cytokinins promote cell division.|T|Yes."])
C("reproduction-in-plants-and-animals","Meena saw a potato sprout and a seed sprout. \"Plants multiply in more than one way,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Vegetative reproduction produces a new plant from a part like a stem, root or leaf, and asexual reproduction forms new individuals without gametes, as by spores or budding. Sexual reproduction in flowering plants happens in the flower, with male stamens and female carpels, and the ovule contains the egg.",
  "Pollination is the transfer of pollen from anther to stigma, either self or cross, with agents such as wind, water, insects and birds. After pollination, fertilisation unites the male gamete and the egg, forming the zygote and then seed and fruit.",
  "In humans, the male reproductive organs include the testes and the female includes the ovary. Gametogenesis is the making of sperms and ova, the menstrual cycle is the monthly cycle in females, and fertilisation is followed by foetal development."],
 ["vegetative reproduction","pollination","fertilisation","gametogenesis","menstrual cycle","zygote"],
 ("What is pollination?",["Pollen is made in the anther.","It must reach the stigma.","This transfer is pollination."]),
 ("Fertilisation happens before pollination.",["Pollination comes first.","Then the pollen tube leads to fertilisation."]),
 ["Vegetative reproduction uses plant parts.","Pollination moves pollen to the stigma.","Fertilisation makes a zygote.","Gametogenesis makes gametes."],
 ["m|Transfer of pollen to the stigma is|pollination|fertilisation|germination|By wind or insects.","t|Stamens are male parts.|T|Yes.","m|Fusion of male and female gametes is|fertilisation|pollination|digestion|Forms a zygote.","t|Potato sprouting is sexual reproduction.|F|It is vegetative.","m|The female reproductive organ producing ova is the|ovary|testis|anther|In humans.","s|What is the fertilised egg called?|zygote|Zygote.","m|Making of gametes is|gametogenesis|digestion|respiration|Spermatogenesis and oogenesis.","t|Insects can be agents of pollination.|T|Yes."])
